ts)  | Clique number of tournaments  | A tournament `H` is `\\operatorname{\\overrightarrow{\\chi}}`-binding if and only if `H` has a backedge graph which is a forest.  | Added in this revision. Refuted by Aubian (arXiv:2401.07776, 2024): the construction produces tournaments whose backedge graph is a forest yet whose clique number is 2 with unbounded dichromatic number — directly contradicting the tournament Gyárfás–Sumner statement. Original review (May 2026) tagged this 'open' because the Aubian abstract did not name which AACL conjecture it refuted; the TeX source identifies it as Conjecture 4.3.\nsolved  | Conjecture 5.3 (Large dom implies an ω̄-cluster)  | Clique number of tournaments  | There exist two functions `f` and `\\ell` such that, for every integer `k`, every tournament `T` with `\\operatorname{dom}(T)\\geq f(k)` contains a subtournament `…  | Conjecture 5.3 asks whether large dom(T) forces a bounded-size subtournament with large clique number ω̄. Crew, Fan, Koerts, Moore, and Spirkl (arXiv:2602.09863, 2026) prove Corollary 7 of their paper, which is exactly Conjecture 5.8 of the…\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2310.04265] Clique number of tournaments (https://accelresearching.com/accel-zinc-picolinate-50mg/?_=%2Fabs%2F2310.04265%23nLw79nwZb1b3uPkZUsgpm5M%3D)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; [Submitted on 6 Oct 2023 (v1), last revised 22 Jun 2026 (this version, v2)] ... A class of tournaments is $\\dic$-bounded if, for every tournament in the class, its dichromatic number is bounded by a function of its clique number. ... We prove that a $\\dic$-bounded class of tournaments satisfies the $BIG \\Rightarrow BIG$ Conjecture, and that a polynomially $\\dic$-bounded class of tournaments satisfies the (tournament) Erdős-Hajnal Conjecture. ... Cite as:  | arXiv:2310.04265 [math.CO]\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 6 Oct 2023 (v1), last revised 22 Jun 2026 (this version, v2)]\n\n# Title:Clique number of tournaments\n\nAuthors:Pierre Aboulker, Guillaume Aubian, Pierre Charbit, Raul Lopes\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Clique number of tournaments, by Pierre Aboulker and 3 other authors\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:Given a digraph $D$ together with an ordering $\\prec$ of its vertices, the \\emph{backedge graph} of $D$ with respect to $\\prec$ is the undirected graph $D^{\\prec}$ with the same vertex set as $D$, where $xy \\in E(D^{\\prec})$ if $xy \\in A(D)$ and $y \\prec x$. We introduce the notion of the \\emph{clique number of a digraph} $D$, defined as the minimum clique number over all backedge graphs of $D$. We investigate its relationship with the dichromatic number. In particular, this concept allows us to define $\\dic$-bounded classes of digraphs, which constitute the main topic of this paper, with a primary focus on tournaments. A class of tournaments is $\\dic$-bounded if, for every tournament in the class, its dichromatic number is bounded by a function of its clique number. We study for which tournaments $H$ the class of $H$-free tournaments is $\\dic$-bounded, and prove in particular that $H$ must have a backedge graph that is a forest. We prove that if a class of tournaments is $\\dic$-bounded, then so is its closure under substitution. We also explore the relationship between $\\dic$-bounded classes of tournaments and certain conjectures on tournaments. We prove that a $\\dic$-bounded class of tournaments satisfies the $BIG \\Rightarrow BIG$ Conjecture, and that a polynomially $\\dic$-bounded class of tournaments satisfies the (tournament) Erdős-Hajnal Conjecture.\n\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO); Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)\nMSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 05C20\nCite as:  | arXiv:2310.04265 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2310.04265v2 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.04265 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Pierre Aboulker [view email]\n[v1] Fri, 6 Oct 2023 14:11:26 UTC (31 KB)\n[v2] Mon, 22 Jun 2026 10:06:06 UTC (31 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe infinite critical tournaments conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/360254/the-infinite-critical-tournaments-conjecture)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; A tournament $T$T is $k$k-clique-critical if $\\operatorname{\\overrightarrow{\\omega}}(T)=k$ω(T)=k and $\\operatorname{\\overrightarrow{\\omega}}(T-v)=k-1$ω(T−v)=k−1 for every $v\\in V(T)$v∈V(T). ... The paper explains that this conjecture is tied to the stronger cluster question: if true it would refute that question, while if false it would support it; the conjecture itself remains open. ... Pierre Aboulker, Guillaume Aubian, Pierre Charbit and Raul Lopes, “Clique number of tournaments”, arXiv:2310.04265 (2026).\n\n# The infinite critical tournaments conjecture\n\n3 years old · traced to 2023\n\nA tournament $T$T is $k$k-clique-critical if $\\operatorname{\\overrightarrow{\\omega}}(T)=k$ω(T)=k and $\\operatorname{\\overrightarrow{\\omega}}(T-v)=k-1$ω(T−v)=k−1 for every $v\\in V(T)$v∈V(T). The infinite critical tournaments conjecture. For every integer $k\\geq 3$k≥3, there is an infinite number of $k$k-clique-critical tournaments. The paper explains that this conjecture is tied to the stronger cluster question: if true it would refute that question, while if false it would support it; the conjecture itself remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Pierre Aboulker, Guillaume Aubian, Pierre Charbit and Raul Lopes, “Clique number of tournaments”, arXiv:2310.04265 (2026).\n\nclique-numbercritical-structurestournaments\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\nNo solutions