onths ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2310.04265 ... A class of tournaments is \\dic-bounded if, for every tournament in the class, its dichromatic number is bounded by a function of its clique number. ... arXiv:2310.04265v2 [math.CO] 22 Jun 2026 ... The analogue of Theorem 5.1 for clique number is Conjecture 5.3 and indeed we have the following implication.\n\nPreprint PDF Available\n\n# Clique number of tournaments\n\n  * June 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2310.04265\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nPierre Aboulker\n\n  * Paris Diderot University\n\nGuillaume Aubian\n\nGuillaume Aubian\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nPierre Charbit\n\n  * Université Paris Cité\n\nRaul Lopes\n\nRaul Lopes\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nGiven a digraph D together with an ordering $\\prec$ of its vertices, the \\emph{backedge graph} of D with respect to $\\prec$ is the undirected graph $D^{\\prec}$ with the same vertex set as D, where $xy \\in E(D^{\\prec})$ if $xy \\in A(D)$ and $y \\prec x$. We introduce the notion of the \\emph{clique number of a digraph} D, defined as the minimum clique number over all backedge graphs of D. \nGiven an undirected graph G, we denote by ω(G)the size of a maximum clique of G and by χ(G)its chromatic\n\nnumber. Given a digraph D, we denote by −→\n\nχ(D)its dichromatic number, that is the minimum integer k such that\n\nthe set of vertices of D can be partitioned into k acyclic subdigraphs.\n\n1\n\narXiv:2310.04265v2 [math.CO] 22 Jun 2026\n\nThe dichromatic number was ﬁrst introduced by Neumann-Lara [39] in 1982 and rediscovered by Mohar [37]\n\n20 years later. It is easy to see that for any undirected graph G, the symmetric digraph ←→\n\nG obtained from G\n\nby replacing each edge by a digon satisﬁes χ(G) = −→\n\nχ(←→\n\nG). This simple fact permits generalizing results\n\non the chromatic number of undirected graphs to digraphs via the dichromatic number. Such results have (re-\n\ncently) been found in various areas of graph colouring such as extremal graph theory [8, 30, 35], algebraic graph\n\ntheory [38], substructure forced by large dichromatic number [1, 2, 3, 11, 22, 25, 46], list dichromatic num-\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMansfield, S., & Mudgal, A. (2023). A Quadratic Vinogradov Mean (https://research-information.bris.ac.uk/files/402789709/2310.02950v2.pdf)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.02950 ... 10.48550/arXiv.2310.02950\nMansfield, S., & Mudgal, A. (2023). A Quadratic Vinogradov Mean\nValue Theorem in Finite Fields.\nhttps://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.02950\nEarly version, also known as pre-print\nLicense (if available):\nUnspecified\nLink to published version (if available):\n10.48550/arXiv.2310.02950\nLink to publication record on the Bristol Research Portal\nPDF-document\nUniversity of Bristol – Bristol Research Portal\nGeneral rights\nThis document is made available in accordance with publisher policies. Please cite only the\npublished version using the reference above. Full terms of use are available:\nhttp://www.bristol.ac.uk/red/research-policy/pure/user-guides/brp-terms/--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStrong Erdős–Hajnal property for the directed triangle | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/373422/strong-erdos-hajnal-property-for-the-directed-triangle)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Let the directed triangle mean the cyclic tournament on three vertices, and say that a tournament has the Strong Erdős–Hajnal property when it satisfies the strong Erdős–Hajnal conjecture. ... 2 papers in this index state this conjecture (2023–2026). The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are arXiv:2310.04265.\n\n# Strong Erdős–Hajnal property for the directed triangle\n\n3 years old · traced to 2023\n\nLet the directed triangle mean the cyclic tournament on three vertices, and say that a tournament has the Strong Erdős–Hajnal property when it satisfies the strong Erdős–Hajnal conjecture. Directed-triangle conjecture. The directed triangle has the Strong Erdős–Hajnal property.\n\nThis is presented as a special case of the strong Erdős–Hajnal conjecture. The supplied text does not state whether this special case has been resolved.\n\nReferences\n\n2 papers in this index state this conjecture (2023–2026). The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are arXiv:2310.04265.\n\nSource: Ararat Harutyunyan, Colin McDiarmid and Gil Puig i Surroca, “Acyclic sets and colorings in digraphs under restrictions on degrees and cycle lengths”, arXiv:2603.02947 (2026).\n\ndigraphsdirected-trianglesstrong-erdos-hajnal\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExistence conjecture for binary upcycles of diamondicity one | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/360427/existence-conjecture-for-binary-upcycles-of-diamondicity-one)\nciteturn2search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The question arises because all currently known upcycles can be constructed starting from binary upcycles with $d=1$d=1, and the divisibility condition $n\\mid da^{n-d}$n∣d a n−d forces a binary upcycle with $d=1$d=1 to have $n=2^k$n=2 k for some $k\\ge2$k≥2. ... Dylan Fillmore, Bennet Goeckner, Rachel Kirsch, Kirin Martin and Daniel McGinnis, “The Existence and Structure of Universal Partial Cycles”, arXiv:2310.13067 (2